Ship the lists a guild should not have to write itself

Every guild that installs this platform writes the same first twenty filter
rules by hand: free-Nitro scams, gift-card bait, IP-logger domains, unsolicited
invites. Doing that one `/wordlist-add` at a time — five slash options per rule,
"added" as the only reply — is how a server ends up with three near-miss
variants of one scam pattern and a gap where the fourth should be.

Packaged lists, layered rather than seeded.

`packages/moderation/src/wordlist-packs.ts` ships three: Scam bait, Risky links
and Hypixel risk. The last is the one that justifies the mechanism at all — a
general-purpose Discord filter list knows nothing about account selling, coins
for PayPal or named cheat clients, and those are the messages a SkyBlock guild
most needs caught, not because they are offensive but because a guild whose
chat carries them attracts Hypixel's attention.

Every pack is off until an admin turns it on: installing this platform changes
nothing about what a guild filters. And a pack is layered *under* the guild's
own rules rather than copied into them, so enabling one adds no rows, a release
that fixes a pattern fixes it for everyone who had the pack on, and switching it
off returns the guild's own list untouched. Seeding rows at install would make a
pack unfixable the moment a scam changed shape, because by then it is a thousand
guilds' data.

Risky links flags rather than blocks throughout, except the IP loggers. A
shortener is not proof of anything, and a filter that silently eats every bit.ly
teaches members the server is broken.

No packaged slur list, and that is the decision, not an omission.

Which words a community treats as a slur is specific to its language, its region
and its membership. A list written here would be simultaneously too aggressive
for some guilds and full of holes for others, and every guild that enabled it
would stop looking — which is worse than shipping nothing. Import is how a guild
brings the list its own moderators agree on. The reasoning is in the module doc
so the next person to add one reads it first.

Suppression is per rule, which is what "extending a packaged list" means.

A guild that wants Risky links but posts bit.ly links internally should not have
to choose between a rule that fires on its own staff and losing the IP loggers
too. Muting `links:shortener` keeps the rest, and the mute outlives switching the
pack off and on again — a guild that already decided against a rule should not
silently get it back a week later.

One evaluator, not two.

`packRules` emits the same `WordlistRuleDTO` a typed rule is, and
`WordlistServiceImpl.list` resolves packs into its answer, so the automod runner,
`/filter-test` and the panel all see one list. `WordlistFilterImpl` in the bridge
resolves them into its own cache for the same reason: a guild that switches on
the Hypixel pack means it for guild chat above all, since that is where account
selling actually gets typed. A filter that behaved differently depending on where
a rule came from would be a second engine to keep in step.

Both reads fail toward the guild's own rules. A settings hiccup that turned every
enabled pack off at once, silently, is the failure worth engineering against —
and in the relay it would be cached that way for a full TTL.

Only the panel tells the two apart, and only so `update` and `remove` can decline
a row this repository owns rather than offering a button that cannot work.

JSON import, validated whole.

Up to 200 rules, pasted or dropped in as a file. Every row is checked before any
row is written, so a bad regex at line ninety rejects the file rather than
leaving eighty-nine rules and a question about which ones landed. Rows that
already exist are skipped rather than refused — an import is usually the second
import, and failing on the overlap would make the feature useless for its one
job. The file picker fills the box rather than posting: seeing what you are about
to add is the entire safeguard on a bulk write. The 200 ceiling is not storage,
it is the number past which nobody has read what they are uploading.

`/wordlist`, `/wordlist-add` and `/wordlist-remove` are retired.

`enabled: false`, so they leave Discord's registry on the next deploy instead of
sitting in the picker answering with an error. The handlers stay and stay under
test. Parity was confirmed before the flag went on, which is why the removal and
the panel work are one slice: the Filter section already had create, edit, delete
and list, and now has the two things the commands could never do. The admin
transport test grew the same both-halves assertion the bridge already had — that
a retired command is absent from the payload, not merely present and erroring.

Audit trails still count rows without reproducing patterns. The wordlist is the
one piece of guild config whose contents *are* the scam URLs and slurs it exists
to catch, and a trail that reproduces every one of them is a second copy of the
thing nobody wanted written down.

Gates: tsc -b clean, client build clean, 2291 tests pass, embeds 0 errors,
brand layer clean.

